::GEO_U03_PT_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 46&deg; and 59&deg;. What is the third angle? {
=A. 75&deg;#Correct. Triangle interior angles sum to 180&deg;, so 180 - 46 - 59 = 75.
~B. 85&deg;#This does not make the three angles total 180&deg;. Recheck the Triangle Angle Sum Theorem.
~C. 105&deg;#This is the sum of the two given angles, not the missing angle. Subtract the known angles from 180&deg;.
~D. 65&deg;#This subtracts incorrectly. The known angles sum to 105&deg;, leaving 75&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle of a triangle is 128&deg;. One remote interior angle is 53&deg;. What is the other remote interior angle? {
~A. 181&deg;#This adds the exterior angle and the remote interior angle. The Exterior Angle Theorem says the remote interior angles add to the exterior angle.
=B. 75&deg;#Correct. By the Exterior Angle Theorem, 53 + x = 128, so x = 75.
~C. 65&deg;#This would make the remote interior angles sum to 118&deg;, not 128&deg;.
~D. 52&deg;#This confuses the exterior angle relationship with a linear-pair subtraction. Use 53 + x = 128.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which triangle congruence criterion uses two pairs of corresponding sides and the included angle? {
~A. SSS#SSS uses three pairs of corresponding sides. It does not include an angle.
~B. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the included side. The question gives two sides and an included angle.
=C. SAS#Correct. SAS uses two pairs of corresponding sides and the included angle.
~D. AAA#AAA does not prove triangle congruence because triangles can have the same angles but different sizes.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which information is not enough to prove two triangles congruent? {
~A. Three pairs of corresponding sides#Three pairs of corresponding sides support SSS, which proves triangle congruence.
~B. Two angles and the included side#Two angles and the included side support ASA, which proves triangle congruence.
~C. Hypotenuse and one leg in right triangles#Hypotenuse-leg information supports HL for right triangles.
=D. Three pairs of corresponding angles#Correct. AAA can show the same shape, but it does not guarantee the same size, so it does not prove congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.3<br><br>Question:<br>Why can valid triangle congruence criteria be connected to rigid transformations? {
=A. They give enough evidence to map one triangle exactly onto the other without changing size or shape.#Correct. Valid congruence criteria support a rigid-motion match because rigid transformations preserve distance and angle measure.
~B. They allow one triangle to be stretched until it matches the other.#Stretching changes distance, so it is not a rigid transformation and does not justify congruence.
~C. They depend only on the triangles looking the same.#Appearance is not proof. Congruence criteria use mathematical evidence, not visual guessing.
~D. They prove only that the triangles have equal areas.#Equal area alone does not prove triangle congruence. Congruence requires same size and shape.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.3<br><br>Question:<br>A reflection followed by a translation maps triangle ABC exactly onto triangle DEF. What conclusion is supported? {
~A. The triangles are similar but not congruent.#Reflections and translations are rigid transformations, so they preserve exact size and shape.
=B. Triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF#Correct. A sequence of rigid transformations that maps one triangle exactly onto another supports triangle congruence.
~C. The triangles must be different sizes.#Rigid transformations preserve size, so this conclusion contradicts the evidence.
~D. No conclusion can be made.#A rigid-motion mapping is valid evidence for congruence, so a conclusion can be made.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.2<br><br>Question:<br>In a parallelogram, what is true about opposite sides? {
~A. They are always perpendicular.#Perpendicular opposite sides are not a general parallelogram property.
~B. They are always different lengths.#Opposite sides in a parallelogram are congruent, not always different.
=C. They are congruent.#Correct. Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
~D. They have no relationship.#Parallelograms have defined opposite-side relationships.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.2<br><br>Question:<br>The diagonals of a parallelogram intersect at M. If AM = 6 on diagonal AC, what is MC? {
~A. 3#This halves AM, but M is already the midpoint of diagonal AC.
~B. 12#This is the full diagonal AC, not the other half MC.
~C. 18#This triples the known half and does not use the diagonal bisection property.
=D. 6#Correct. Diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so AM = MC.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.3<br><br>Question:<br>A trapezoid has bases 10 and 18. What is the length of its midsegment? {
=A. 14#Correct. The trapezoid midsegment length is the average of the bases: (10 + 18) / 2 = 14.
~B. 8#This subtracts the base lengths. The midsegment uses the average, not the difference.
~C. 28#This adds the bases but does not divide by 2.
~D. 180#This multiplies the bases. The trapezoid midsegment formula does not use multiplication.
}

::GEO_U03_PT_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_PT_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.3<br><br>Question:<br>In an isosceles trapezoid, one base angle measures 72&deg;. What is the measure of the other angle on the same base? {
~A. 108&deg;#This may describe an adjacent supplementary angle, but the question asks for the other angle on the same base.
=B. 72&deg;#Correct. Base angles on the same base of an isosceles trapezoid are congruent.
~C. 36&deg;#This halves the angle without a supporting trapezoid relationship.
~D. 144&deg;#This doubles the angle without using the isosceles trapezoid base-angle property.
}
